From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: allwinner: a733: add Timer1 as the tick broadcast device

What. Describe the A733's Timer1_CPUX (0x02052000) with the sun8i-a23 timer
fallback, and extend the binding to accept "allwinner,sun60i-a733-timer".
timer-sun4i.c then registers timer 0 as a global clockevent ("sun4i_tick",
rating 350, ONESHOT | PERIODIC | DYNIRQ, no C3STOP), and the tick layer
takes it as the broadcast device instead of the hrtimer emulation.

Why. The DT describes only the per-CPU arch timer, and
cpu-sleep-0 stops it (local-timer-stop). With no other clockevent,
arm64's time_init() installs the hrtimer broadcast (bc_hrtimer, rating 0).
The CPU its hrtimer is pinned to gets -EBUSY from tick_broadcast_enter()
(broadcast_needs_cpu(), kernel/time/tick-broadcast.c), and
cpuidle_enter_state() then puts it in WFI (drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle.c).
Measured at idle: 1.5-1.7 CPUs of idle time go to WFI this
way (6 GiB board). Under a fixed 50 % injection, one A55 per run
injected all of its idle time in WFI (8 GiB board). A clockevent
without C3STOP and not per-CPU outranks bc_hrtimer
(tick_check_broadcast_device()), and broadcast_needs_cpu() returns 0 for
anything that is not the hrtimer emulation.

Where each value comes from (A733 UM V1.00, original PDF):
  * 8.2.6 register list, PDF p. 386: Timer1_CPUX base 0x02052000;
    TMR_IRQ_EN 0x00, TMR_IRQ_STA 0x04, TMRn_CTRL / INTV_VALUE / CUR_VALUE
    at 0x10 / 0x14 / 0x18 + 0x10 * n for n = 0..3. The memory map (PDF p. 25)
    gives the same base, 4K.
  * 8.2.7.3 TMR0_CTRL_REG, PDF p. 388: bit 7 mode (1 = single), 6:4
    prescaler, 3:2 source (00 RTC_32K, 01 SYS_CLK24M, reset 01), bit 1
    reload, bit 0 enable. 8.2.7.2: status bits are W1C. These are
    timer-sun4i.c's TIMER_CTL_ONESHOT / CLK_PRES / CLK_SRC / RELOAD /
    ENABLE, TIMER_IRQ_EN_REG / TIMER_IRQ_ST_REG and TIMER_CTL/INTVAL/
    CNTVAL_REG(n), bit for bit. The driver uses timer 0 (clockevent) and
    timer 1 (32-bit down-counting clocksource, rating 350, below
    arch_sys_counter's 400). Timers 2 and 3 stay free.
  * Interrupt table, PDF p. 616: INTID 131 TIMER1_0, 132 TIMER1_1, 133-134
    TIMER1_2/3, so GIC SPI 99-102.
    The binding's sun8i-a23 clause takes exactly two interrupts, the same
    as the A64/H6/H616 nodes. The driver requests only the first.
  * Clock: 8.2.3 and 8.2.7.3 name the two count clocks SYS_CLK24M (01) and
    RTC_32K (00), selected in CTRL[3:2]; the driver writes 01. The CCU
    register list has TIMER0_CLKn and TIMER0_BGR_REG but no gate or reset
    for Timer1, so the node has no resets property.

Silicon, 8 GiB board, 2026-09-24, test kernel (no driver bound),
busybox devmem:
  * Readback 0x02052000..0x0205204b: IRQ_EN 0, IRQ_STA 0, TMR0..3_CTRL
    0x00000004 each (the documented reset value, at four offsets), every
    INTV and CUR 0. Boot0, BL31 and U-Boot leave the block untouched, and
    it answers non-secure reads.
  * Stimulus on timer 3, which the driver never uses: INTV = 0xffffffff,
    CTRL = source | reload | enable. The writes stick (reload self-clears:
    0x7 reads back 0x5), and the counter runs down. Against the board's own
    CLOCK_REALTIME (arch_sys_counter, NTP-synchronised, 8 ms from the host)
    over 2 s: source 01 counts at 25.996 MHz, source 00 at 32.8 kHz; over
    10 s on the test kernel 26.001 MHz and 32 769 Hz. So the
    input the UM calls SYS_CLK24M is the 26 MHz DCXO on this SoC -- the
    24-versus-26-MHz defect type. The node names &dcxo26M, the board's DCXO
    fixed-clock (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-feed-pll-ref-from-the-dcxo`), which has its rate at time_init() through
    of_clk_init(); the RTC-CCU's CLK_RTC_DCXO would come from a platform
    driver, after time_init(). With &sys24M every event would fire 1/13
    early and the "timer" clocksource would run 8 % fast. Interrupt enable
    stayed 0; timer 3 was stopped and restored.

Rejected: TIMER0 at 0x03009000, the address where the H6 has its a23-style
timer. On the A733 that address holds a different block: 56-bit counters,
TIMER_CTRL_REG at 0x20 + 0x20 * N, and TIMER_SEC_REG resets to 0x3FF, so
every timer is secure (8.1.6-8.1.7.3, PDF p. 379-381). No mainline driver
fits it, and BL31 would have to release it first. Nothing is taken from the
A523, whose mainline DT has no timer node. The node sits between the CCU and
the eFuse, not after WDT0 at 0x02050000 where address order puts it: `arm64-dts-a733-cubie-a7s-watchdog-on-wdt-cpus`
carries the WDT0 node's closing lines as context, and an insertion there
breaks `arm64-dts-a733-cubie-a7s-watchdog-on-wdt-cpus` at fuzz 0.

Vendor: the 6.6 BSP drives TIMER0 at 0x03009000 through its own driver
("allwinner,sun50i-timer", bus gate, reset, two module clocks, one
interrupt) and does not describe Timer1. Nothing is taken from it; Timer1
needs no vendor code, only the mainline driver.

Verified on silicon, 8 GiB board, 2026-09-24, test kernel 6.18.36-t9997
against the default kernel without this patch on the same image, each booted by label:
  * broadcast/current_device reads sun4i_tick; /proc/timer_list shows it as
    the broadcast device and no bc_handler; "timer@2052000" (GICv3 131)
    counts on all eight CPUs; clocksource stays arch_sys_counter.
  * Idle, 2 x 60 s: 0 tick_broadcast_oneshot_control() refusals (default
    kernel 7131-7751); deep share 89.5-99.9 % on every CPU (default 18-95 %);
    WFI idle time 0.35 CPUs (default 2.0-2.2 CPUs).
  * Injection at 50 % into cpu-sleep-0, 2 x 90 s: every injected core 100 %
    in cpu-sleep-0, 0 refusals. Default kernel: the broadcast hrtimer's
    owner put 73 % and 100 % of its injected idle in WFI.
  * Wake, traced, 8 000 absolute 20 ms sleeps per kernel: a deep core is
    woken by the Timer1 interrupt directly; hrtimer expiry to handler
    170 / 217 us (A55 / A76 median) against 169 / 212 us for the default
    kernel's deep wakes by the broadcast IPI. 88-98 % of the sleeps now go
    deep (default 1-5 %), so the median wake to the task rises from ~95 / 32
    us (WFI) to 262 / 281 us, the deep state's own cost. No wake is late in
    the kernel; the two sleeps over 1 ms were WFI sleeps whose task waited
    behind another one until the next tick.
  * timesyncd after 4 packets, two boots: offset +2.05 / +0.42 ms, frequency
    -4.1 / +2.7 ppm. No WARN, RCU stall or lockup.

For a comparison run without it: A7S_SKIP_PATCHES=arm64-dts-a733-add-timer1-as-the-tick-broadcast-device.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/timer/allwinner,sun4i-a10-timer.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/timer/allwinner,sun4i-a10-timer.yaml
@@ -24,6 +24,7 @@
               - allwinner,sun50i-a64-timer
               - allwinner,sun50i-h6-timer
               - allwinner,sun50i-h616-timer
+              - allwinner,sun60i-a733-timer
           - const: allwinner,sun8i-a23-timer
 
   reg:
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -551,6 +551,50 @@
 		};
 
 		/*
+		 * Timer1_CPUX, the tick broadcast device. The arch timer stops in
+		 * cpu-sleep-0 (local-timer-stop). Without a clockevent that keeps
+		 * running there, the kernel emulates the broadcast with an
+		 * hrtimer, and whichever CPU holds that hrtimer is refused deep
+		 * idle. This block is a SoC peripheral with its own SPIs, not
+		 * part of any core, so a core power-down does not stop it.
+		 *
+		 * reg     A733 UM V1.00 8.2.6 (PDF p. 386): base 0x02052000;
+		 *         TMR_IRQ_EN 0x00, TMR_IRQ_STA 0x04 (W1C), TMRn_CTRL,
+		 *         INTV and CUR at 0x10, 0x14, 0x18 + 0x10 * n, n = 0..3.
+		 *         0x50 covers the header and all four timers.
+		 * compat  8.2.7.3 (PDF p. 388): CTRL bit 7 single mode, 6:4
+		 *         prescaler, 3:2 source (01 = SYS_CLK24M), 1 reload,
+		 *         0 enable. That is the layout timer-sun4i.c drives for
+		 *         the sun8i-a23 fallback: timer 0 is the clockevent,
+		 *         timer 1 a free-running clocksource.
+		 * irq     Interrupt table, PDF p. 616: INTID 131 TIMER1_0 and
+		 *         132 TIMER1_1, GIC SPI 99 and 100. The a23 binding
+		 *         takes two; the driver uses the first.
+		 * clocks  The driver selects source 01, which 8.2.3 and 8.2.7.3
+		 *         call SYS_CLK24M. On silicon it counts at 26 MHz, so it
+		 *         is the DCXO (timer 3 read back against the host clock
+		 *         on the 8 GiB board: 25.996 MHz with 01, 32.8 kHz with
+		 *         00 = RTC_32K). Hence &dcxo26M, which is a fixed-clock
+		 *         and so has a rate at time_init(), before the RTC-CCU
+		 *         probes. With &sys24M every event would fire 1/13
+		 *         early. The CCU has no gate or reset for this block;
+		 *         its TIMER0_BGR_REG belongs to the other one.
+		 *
+		 * Not TIMER0 at 0x03009000, the address of the H6's a23-style
+		 * timer: on the A733 that is a different block (56-bit
+		 * counters, CTRL at 0x20 + 0x20 * n, every timer secure out of
+		 * reset; UM 8.1.6-8.1.7.3, PDF p. 379-381).
+		 */
+		timer@2052000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-timer",
+				     "allwinner,sun8i-a23-timer";
+			reg = <0x0 0x02052000 0x0 0x50>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 99 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				     <GIC_SPI 100 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			clocks = <&dcxo26M>;
+		};
+
+		/*
 		 * Security ID / eFuse array. Register-compatible with the A64
 		 * layout: the fuse array is mirrored into the ARM window at
 		 * base + 0x200, plain MMIO reads (no H3-style PRCTL readout).
